    "body": "## Summary\n\n- `mini-frame-mode` alone hung Emacs on macOS (98% CPU in `M-x`), even in the plain setup of #134 and with `vertico-resize` off, so `sp-mini-frame` now uses `vertico-posframe` instead.\n- The minibuffer (prompt and Vertico's candidates) is a centred posframe: `vertico-posframe-poshandler` is `posframe-poshandler-frame-center`, `-min-width` 80, `-border-width` 4 (padding). No fixed height: the frame fits Vertico's own 13 candidates (`vertico-count` in `sp-completion`), so there is no blank box.\n- Terminals fall back to `vertico-buffer-mode`.\n- The module name and the dimming for `find-file`, `vulpea-find` and `org-node-find` stay (the dimming now checks `vertico-posframe-mode`).\n- Docs (`sp-mini-frame.org`, dividers guide, `docs/main.org`) and CHANGELOG updated. The `mini-frame` package is no longer installed; Elpaca installs `vertico-posframe` and `posframe` on the next start.\n\n## Not tested\n\n- Not run in Emacs (none in the container), and the option names come from reading the `vertico-posframe` README.\n- Whether the M-x text is visible and the hang is gone on macOS is unconfirmed.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01MfEFZ7KDkwwcifErLfjf4X)_",
